  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/5cd38fb898d5cc3fcef43ae6397b02f22fbd06cbe598263fe77ce6b936c16bae.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $216$ square units. Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $x.$",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot x\\cdot y={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $216$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base) }+4\\,\\text{(area of  one side) } \\\\ 216 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation: \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =54x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-18<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=6$ units.",
